issues.problematic_code_snippet:
static async hasPendingInviteByUserId({ userId }: { userId: number }): Promise<boolean> {
    const pendingInvite = await prisma.membership.findFirst({
      where: {
        userId,
        accepted: true,
      },
      select: {
        id: true,
      },
    });
    return !!pendingInvite;
  }

// Determine which onboarding path to use
  const onboardingV3Enabled = await featuresRepository.checkIfFeatureIsEnabledGlobally("onboarding-v3");

  const hasPendingInvite = await MembershipRepository.hasPendingInviteByUserId({ userId });

  if (hasPendingInvite || onboardingV3Enabled) {
    return "/onboarding/personal/settings";
  }

  return onboardingV3Enabled ? "/onboarding/getting-started" : "/getting-started";

findings.body:
The new static `hasPendingInviteByUserId` looks for memberships with `accepted: true`, so it returns true for users who already belong to a team. Its name, the PR description, and the query it replaces in `checkOnboardingRedirect` all mean `accepted: false`.

Because the page-level redirect in `getting-started/page.tsx` and `checkOnboardingRedirect` both call this method, the effect is broad. Any user with an accepted team membership is sent to `/onboarding/personal/settings`, and users with only a pending invite do not match at all. The integration tests cover exactly these two cases and would fail against this implementation.

::diff{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.ts" lines="584-595"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The `accepted: true` on line 588 should be `accepted: false`. Please fix this before anything else in this review, because the other findings build on this check.

The original code only sent users to personal onboarding when they had a pending invite AND onboarding-v3 was enabled: `if (pendingInvite && onboardingV3Enabled)`. The PR changes this to `hasPendingInvite || onboardingV3Enabled`.

That means every user who reaches this point, including users with no invite at all, is routed to `/onboarding/personal/settings` as soon as onboarding-v3 is on. They skip the plan-selection step (`/onboarding/getting-started`) entirely. This is a routing change for all new users, not only invitees, and the PR description never mentions it.

::diff{file="packages/features/auth/lib/onboardingUtils.ts" lines="64-74"}

::callout{type="security"}
The PR description says the fix only targets invited users. This change affects all users when onboarding-v3 is enabled. Revert to `&&`, or justify the broader routing change explicitly.

The test file was added to prove the new method works, but its assertions contradict the implementation. The pending-only test expects `true` and the accepted-only test expects `false`. With the `accepted: true` filter in place, the first returns `false` and the second returns `true`, so both fail.

The mixed-membership test is also weaker than it looks. Its pending membership is only created `if (team2)`, and the final assertion is `expect(result).toBe(team2 ? true : false)`. If the database has no second team, the test expects `false` for a user that has a pending invite in the title's scenario. The assertion therefore depends on the test data rather than the behavior under test, and the test can pass for the wrong reason.

::code{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.integration-test.ts" lines="119-162"}

::suggestion{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.integration-test.ts" lines="144-158"}
// Create a dedicated second team in beforeAll/in the test so the pending membership always exists,
// then assert unconditionally:
expect(result).toBe(true);

The rest of `MembershipRepository` takes its client through the constructor (`constructor(private readonly prismaClient: PrismaClient = prisma)`) and uses `this.prismaClient`. The new `hasPendingInviteByUserId` is static and calls the module-level `prisma` directly. That breaks the injection pattern the PR is meant to follow and makes the method impossible to unit-test with a mock or a transaction client.

::diff{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.ts" lines="584-595"}

The PR description says the goal is to avoid direct prisma access outside repositories, and this is inside the repository. But it still bypasses the instance client, so it is inconsistent with the class's other methods.

Each test creates a user and then deletes it at the end of the test body, without `try/finally`. If an assertion fails, the user and any membership it created remain in the test database. The `afterEach` hook only clears memberships tracked in `createdMembershipIds`, and the mixed-membership test attaches memberships to a team it did not create, so a failure can leave data that breaks later runs.

::code{file="packages/features/membership/repositories/MembershipRepository.integration-test.ts" lines="50-75"}

The fix is to track created users in the same way as memberships, or to move the cleanup into `afterEach` with `try/finally`.
